    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Roles are swimlanes",
      "prov:value": "| object | what it is | declared in |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Actor** | a concrete participant. Human, agentic or mechanical. Persists across every process. | `cat-harness/scenarios/actors/*.json` (the declared `scenarios` graph, beside the roles; `.claude/skills/actors/` until 2026-09-30, bean `rqao`) |\n| **Role** | **the swimlane** — a persona an actor *takes on* because of the lane it is acting in. Carries a collection of Skills. | `scenarios/roles.json` |\n| **Skill** | an instruction body: what the actor needs to know to perform the task it was handed. | `skills/<pkg>/*.md` (naming its `input:`/`output:` contracts, usually under `schemas/skills/<name>/`); JSON `SkillDefinition`s in each owning instance's `skills/skill-definitions/` (bean `rqao`) |\n| **Process / Decision** | BPMN and DMN. Lanes bind roles; activities name skills; gateways may compute their branch from a table. | `processes/**/*.bpmn`, `processes/**/decisions/*.dmn` |\n| **Requirement** | a conformance obligation, **pointed at** by what discharges it: a skill or capability names the statement in `satisfies: req:<id>#<key>`. The requirement points at `actors` (who is bound) and `derivedFrom` (the broader requirement it specialises). | `skills/requirements/*.json` |\n| **Permission** | what an actor is **allowed to do**. Cross-cuts roles. A W3C ODRL 2.2 rule, scoped by Process, Task or Role when it needs to be (issue #1180). | actions: `skills/permissions/permissions.json`; who holds them: `policies/*.jsonld` |",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean rqao",
        "#1180",
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Roles are swimlanes",
      "prov:value": "**WHO's \"actor\" is this model's Role.** An actor in the WHO DPI-H Reference\nArchitecture's conformance model (§3.7.2, an abstract information-processing\nrole) and a DAK generic persona each map to a folio-assistant **Role**, never\nto an Actor. The RA's system *realising* an actor maps to a mechanical Actor\ntaking on that role. Owner, 2026-10-03 (#1984); the comparison is on\nthe DAK layer's `findings/dth-terms.md`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1984",
        "2026-10-03"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-03"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Not every skill is PERFORMED — `consulted: true`",
      "prov:value": "**Why it had to be declared.** `kg-audit`'s `skill-in-role-or-process`\ncounted both kinds together. Two mechanical discriminators were tested over\nthe unbound set (bean `y1w9`) and both cut across the distinction rather\nthan along it: a sibling `.ts` `SkillDefinition` covers a fifth of them and\nmisses `glossary-build` and `editor`; an `allowed-tools:` line covers half\nand splits the same families arbitrarily. The distinction is real,\nload-bearing for a criterion, and was declared nowhere.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean y1w9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-4",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Not every skill is PERFORMED — `consulted: true`",
      "prov:value": "### A skill's `roles:` front matter is GONE — do not bring it back"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-5",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A skill's `roles:` front matter is GONE — do not bring it back",
      "prov:value": "**Removed 2026-09-20**, bean `folio-assistant-qif9`, on the owner's\ninstruction. 114 skill files lost it; the full record, with every value every\nfile carried, is `fsh-guts/retired/skill-roles-front-matter.md` — a path\nrather than a link, because the trashcan is not published and a link from\nhere would break on the site.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean qif9",
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-6",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A skill's `roles:` front matter is GONE — do not bring it back",
      "prov:value": "- **Nothing read it — with one instructive exception.** 0 of 180 Skill nodes\n  in the exported graph carried any role-ish key; the only 27 carriers were\n  `Actor` nodes, from actor JSON, and `gen-skill-docs` dropped it. The\n  exception was `src/impact/stakeholder-map.ts`, which printed it as\n  *\"Roles reached\"* — beside real lane-derived roles, with nothing marking\n  which was which. It now derives roles from the **lanes** it already\n  computes, resolved through `roleForLane`, so every value it prints\n  resolves.\n- **`roles:` in a `folio-memory/v1` entry is a DIFFERENT field and stays.**\n  `memoryForRoles` filters on `tags.roles`; the 26 memory entries keep it.\n  The first pass of the excision took all 140 files and `agent-memory.test.ts`\n  caught it — every entry untagged means every lane sees everything. Sharing\n  a key name across graph typologies is not sharing a field, and\n  `check:retired-front-matter` keys its exemption on `$schema`, never on the\n  directory.\n- **It was dangling from its first commit, not from a later rename.** At\n  migration (`2734a70f`, 2026-06-15) the contract read *\"Actor IDs (roles)\n  that may invoke this skill\"*, pointing at `ActorDefinition.id`. No\n  `reader.json`, `collaborator.json` or `owner.json` has ever been added to\n  `.claude/skills/actors/`, in any commit in the repository's history.\n- **The second vocabulary was a collision.** `Role` was later given to the\n  BPMN swimlane, and authors reading the field by its new name wrote\n  swimlane roles into it. At removal, across the 114 skill files: 288\n  annotations, **260 of them — 90 % — resolving to nothing.**",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-06-15"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-06-15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-7",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Why a lane names a role, and the role is not the person",
      "prov:value": "A lane is not itself a role: **a lane names the Role its tasks are performed\nin** (owner, 2026-10-16: *\"Lane names Role, not Actor\"*).",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-16"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-16"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-8",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Why a lane names a role, and the role is not the person",
      "prov:value": "> **The actor registry used to model this backwards, and now does not.**\n> `.claude/skills/actors/*.json` held eighteen entries with an `inherits`\n> chain — `author` inherits `reviewer` inherits `viewer`. That is a **role**\n> lattice wearing an actor's name: \"can review\", \"can push\" are properties of a\n> position, not of a person. Those entries now carry **`roles[]`** — the roles\n> each actor may take on — and the lattice lives in the role graph, where\n> `inherits` means what it says. Six actors were added for participants that\n> had none: the end user, a stakeholder, the onboarding / ingestion / evidence\n> agents, and the CI pipeline.\n>\n> `roles: []` and an **absent** `roles` mean different things. `[]` says the\n> actor takes on no role, which is the honest value for a read-only identity\n> that never appears in a swimlane; absent says nothing has been asserted.\n>\n> The `actor-is-not-a-role` criterion stays even though it now passes, because\n> the next registry written by hand will reach for `inherits` again."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-9",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Why a lane names a role, and the role is not the person",
      "prov:value": "> **Roles played by mechanical actors are real roles** (owner, 2026-10-16).\n> `Work plan (beans)`, `Corpus`, `Publish target`, a build pipeline and the\n> external registries are roles a machine or an outside party plays. Their\n> skills are their **contract**: the responsibilities they must perform, which\n> a test can hold them to — a contract skill names its tests in front matter as\n> `tested-by:`. These roles used to carry `actedUpon: true`, which said they\n> \"take no part\"; the diagrams hand them tasks, so the flag is retired. The QA\n> report lists a mechanical role whose skills name no test as\n> `mechanical-role-untested`, and `role-has-actor` now asks the fair question\n> of what plays the corpus.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-16"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-16"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-10",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Why a lane names a role, and the role is not the person",
      "prov:value": "> **A store is a mechanical role with a `store` block, and is drawn as a data\n> store, not a lane** (owner, 2026-11-01, Q-BR-3 A, Q-DS-1 A to Q-DS-4 A).\n> `work-plan`, `corpus`, `log` and `session-record` keep things rather than\n> do them. Each stays a Role, so its skills are still its contract, and adds\n> `store`: what it `holds` (typology ids), its `api` (one entry per\n> transport, in that transport's own format: `openapi`, `fhir-capability`,\n> `graphql`, `cli` tools, `json-schema-files`, `git`), what it is\n> `realisedBy`, and whether it is `published`. Only a role played by `system`\n> or `external` actors may declare one. The role id is the store id: each\n> layer declares `<bpmn:dataStore id=\"<role id>\">` once in\n> `processes/global-tasks.bpmn`, and a process draws a `dataStoreReference`\n> pointing at it, with `dataInputAssociation` for a read and\n> `dataOutputAssociation` for a write. A system that does its own step\n> (validates, decides, returns a result) keeps a lane; one that only keeps\n> what others write is a data store. The same store is an ArchiMate\n> application component (its interfaces, data objects and services hang off\n> it) and, in a FHIR layer, an `ActorDefinition` of type system whose\n> `capabilities` or `reference` points at its API.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-11-01"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-11-01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-11",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Why a lane names a role, and the role is not the person",
      "prov:value": "> **Some roles perform, but by judgement.** `stakeholder` carries\n> `judgementOnly: true`: somebody really does sign off, deliberates and is\n> accountable — they simply cannot be handed a procedure that produces the\n> answer. That is a different thing from a mechanical role, which has no\n> judgement to exercise and whose skills are a contract it can be tested\n> against.\n>\n> The flag exists because prose did not hold. The role's own summary has said\n> \"carries no skills deliberately: sign-off is a judgement, not a procedure, and\n> a skill here would suggest an agent could supply it\" since the graph was\n> written — and a later pass, reading four `activity-names-skill` findings on\n> that lane, came within one commit of resolving them by giving the role a\n> skill. A decision recorded only in a summary is a decision the next agent\n> re-litigates.\n>\n> Effect: `activity-names-skill` records **n/a** for activities in the lane,\n> which is what lets that criterion gate on the undeclared ones instead of\n> staying advisory for ever."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-12",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "date",
      "cat-harness:section": "An actor is one of three kinds — human, agentic, mechanical",
      "prov:value": "**It was two values until 2026-09-19 and the conflation was the defect.** Every\nentry carried `type`, which read `person` or `system`, and `system` covered an\nLLM agent and a CI runner alike. Measured on `main` that morning: **16 `person`,\n8 `system`** — and those 8 held **five agents and three mechanical services**.\nSo \"which tasks can this actor perform\" had no answer for a third of the\nregistry, because the question turns on judgement and both sides wore one label.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-19"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-13",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Three questions about an actor, and only two are answered on the actor",
      "prov:value": "**What an actor may DO moved off the actor on 2026-09-23** (issue #1180; owner:\n*\"W3C ODRL 2.2 and W3C PROV-O for logging\"*). Every `permissions` list became\none ODRL rule per action, and `schemas/odrl.test.ts` pins that nobody lost or gained\none. What this buys:",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1180",
        "2026-09-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-14",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Three questions about an actor, and only two are answered on the actor",
      "prov:value": "- **Inheritance.** Each action in `permissions.json` says which broader actions\n  it is `includedIn`, ending at ODRL's own (`odrl:display`, `odrl:modify`,\n  `odrl:execute`, …). A grant of a broader action permits everything included\n  in it. The graph need not be a tree and may have cycles.\n- **Scope.** A rule may be limited to a Process, a Task or a Role. No\n  constraint means everywhere.\n- **One question, three answers.** `permits()` in `schemas/content/odrl.ts` returns\n  `permit`, `deny` or `unknown`, and `unknown` is never permit. Performing a\n  task in a lane needs the permission **and** the lane's role, so eligibility\n  (`roles`) and permission stay separate, as below.\n- **Anyone.** `cat-harness:anyone` is an unauthenticated reader. The owner's floor:\n  it may `visualize` and `render`, and nothing else. Owner, 2026-09-24:\n  *\"person acting w/ no login = reader/browser\"*. A person with no login is a\n  reader/browser, whoever they are, and holds exactly this.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-24"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-24"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-15",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Three questions about an actor, and only two are answered on the actor",
      "prov:value": "**Identity is not here.** Which login is which actor is the data store's to\nknow (owner, 2026-09-23). No actor file and no policy carries a login, and\nworkflow history names the **actor**, never the login: the repository owner's\nsteps are recorded as `owner` (owner, 2026-09-24, *\"Yes, exactly\"*), and the\ndata store maps the login to `owner`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-23",
        "2026-09-24"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-23",
        "2026-09-24"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-16",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Three questions about an actor, and only two are answered on the actor",
      "prov:value": "**Who reads all three before a task runs:** the BPMN executor. Before any task\nor decision is recorded, `authorizeTask` asks whether the actor is\nauthenticated, eligible for the lane's role (`roles`), and permitted by policy\nto `perform-task` here and on this content. A role mismatch or a `deny`\nrefuses; `unknown` is recorded while the rollout is advisory. The HTTP routes\nask the same policies through `src/core/rbac.ts`. See\n[`task-authorization`](task-authorization.md), issue #1207.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1207"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-17",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Three questions about an actor, and only two are answered on the actor",
      "prov:value": "**These were one field until 2026-09** (bean `ind9`), and the conflation meant\nnothing could resolve any of them: 27 claims across 19 names pointed at a\ncapability registry that only ever held environment probes.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean ind9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-18",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "Three questions about an actor, and only two are answered on the actor",
      "prov:value": "**Three names were neither**: `cql-authoring`, `data-dictionary-authoring` and\n`lean-diagnostics` are skills with no body anywhere. Dropped rather than\nrelocated — claiming an unmodelled thing is worse than not claiming it, and\nputting them on a role would fail `role-skills-resolve`. Bean `dtod`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean dtod"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-19",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Requirements are the fifth node kind, and they only point",
      "prov:value": "The statement names no satisfier (#1168): a requirement is written once, and\nwhat discharges it arrives later, so the pointer lives on the arrival.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "#1168"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-20",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "date",
      "cat-harness:section": "Requirements are the fifth node kind, and they only point",
      "prov:value": "**They were in `.claude/skills/requirements/` until 2026-09-18**, which is a\nClaude-Code-only directory that Gemini, Cursor and Copilot never read, and their\njoins were unchecked the whole time. Moving them into the `kg` graph found four\nbroken references on the first run: `req:agent-workflow` did not exist although\nthree requirements declared `derivedFrom` it, and `capability:role-detection`\ndid not exist either — capabilities here are environment probes\n(`detection: { method: \"command\" }`), and role detection is the actor registry\nread against the session identity, which is a skill.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-21",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Two compositions, and they are not the same",
      "prov:value": "## A higher instance EXTENDS a role by id — it never edits it (placement PR0b)"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-22",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "A higher instance EXTENDS a role by id — it never edits it (placement PR0b)",
      "prov:value": "**The one exception — a move in flight** (owner, 2026-10-23, \"Relax the\nrule\"). Moving a role, actor, capability or policy up a layer is two pull\nrequests: the higher layer adds it, then the lower layer removes it, and in\nbetween both declare the id. A redeclaration that **only adds** — every field\nthe lower declaration carries is there unchanged (the same scalar, every\nmember of an array, a nested object by the same rule), and it may carry more,\nsuch as the skills it used to add by extension — is accepted with a warning\nthat names the id and both layers, and the higher declaration is used. The\nwarning is the move's to-do: remove the lower copy. A redeclaration that\nchanges or drops anything the lower one said is still refused.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-10-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-10-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-23",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "A higher instance EXTENDS a role by id — it never edits it (placement PR0b)",
      "prov:value": "## Lanes are free text — which is the problem the role graph solves"
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-24",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Lanes are free text — which is the problem the role graph solves",
      "prov:value": "The older `<bootstrap.processes:role ref=\"reviewer\"/>` in the lane's\n`extensionElements` is read for one release, with a warning, and only when\n`partitionElementRef` is absent (`laneRoleRef` in `schemas/process/role-graph.ts`); the\nQA report lists it as `lane-role-legacy-binding`. `<bootstrap.processes:role\nvariable=\"true\"/>` stays: it declares a performer who is whoever invoked the\nprocess. An explicit binding **wins** over name matching: a diagram that has\nsaid which role it means must not be second-guessed by a string table. A ref\nnaming no declared role is a `critical` finding — it is *not* quietly\nname-matched instead."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-25",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "The audit — `bun run cat kg:audit`",
      "prov:value": "One criterion per join in the sentence at the top. The list below is the\nactor→role→skill→task core; **`KG_CRITERIA` in `schemas/sdlc/kg-qa.ts` is the\nregistry**, and it is longer — it also covers requirements, skill bodies and\nthe graph as a whole. This said \"Fourteen criteria\" while the registry held\n**32**, which is the same defect the audit exists to catch, one level up: a\ncount in prose is a claim, and the thing it counts is a file you can read."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-26",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Where the sidecars for another instance go — hosted, not written into it",
      "prov:value": "An instance that declares no `qa` directory does not receive sidecars inside\nits own tree: `kgQaHomeFor` (`schemas/kg/cat-harness.ts`) puts them in the\nauditing harness's `qa` directory under the instance's stub —\n`cat-harness/test/results/bootstrap/` for bootstrap (a working copy, stored\non the `qa-reports` branch with the rest of that `qa` directory; a JUDGEMENT\nabout it is hosted the same way, under `test/attestations/`). A verdict ABOUT an\ninstance is the auditor's output, and bootstrap is the layer that must read\ncleanly with no harness present (owner, 2026-09-29, decision 2 of bean\n`r3gy`). The same holds once bootstrap is its own repository: the verdicts stay\nwith the harness that computed them, against the pinned bootstrap\n([`kg-separation`](../../kg/graph-management/kg-separation.md) §\"The pair\").",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-29"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-29"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-27",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "Never quote a count from prose — run the audit",
      "prov:value": "**A number written in a document is a claim; only a run is evidence.** This has\nalready gone wrong here in the way that is hardest to catch: a finding was\nresolved, the count describing it stayed in `AGENTS.md`, and a later session\nrepeated it back to the author as live."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-28",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "What counts as a skill",
      "prov:value": "**`.claude/skills/` is not uniformly skills**, and reading it as though it were\nwas a live defect: `actors/` holds participants, `capabilities/` holds\nenvironment probes (`docker`, `pandoc`, `python3`), `roles/` holds an assignment\ntable and `hooks/` holds a shell script. Scanning all of them put 46 non-skills\ninto the set, so `<bootstrap.processes:skill ref=\"viewer\"/>` or `ref=\"latex-compiler\"` would\nhave resolved. `NON_SKILL_GROUPS` excludes them — 176 names down to 136, with no\nexisting reference becoming dangling."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-29",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "date",
      "cat-harness:section": "What counts as a skill",
      "prov:value": "## Administration IS a swimlane — settled 2026-09-20",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-30",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Administration IS a swimlane — settled 2026-09-20",
      "prov:value": "**That was a question, not a deduction.** Until the owner answered it, there\nwas an `admin` ACTOR and no `administrator` ROLE, and the honest alternative\nwas live: administration could have been recorded as *a reviewed filesystem\nedit* with no lane of its own. The bean (`folio-assistant-hb2o`) was blocked\non exactly that, and blocked on purpose — **do not invent a role to make a\ndiagram drawable.** That is the closure-widening failure this skill records\nin §\"Two compositions\", arrived at from the other end: a role minted for a\npicture is a role no audit can fail, because nothing was ever true of it."
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-31",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "date",
      "cat-harness:section": "Administration IS a swimlane — settled 2026-09-20",
      "prov:value": "The measurement that made this a *missing process* rather than a broken\nreference, 2026-09-20 across 26 actors, 31 roles and 34 diagrams: 31 of 31\nroles bound a lane, no actor named a role that did not exist, no lane bound\nnothing. One actor had no roles — `viewer`, a **determined** empty, stated\nin its own description. Nothing was dangling. `admin` simply appeared in\nseventeen diagrams as something else every time, and the one thing it\nuniquely was had no picture.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-32",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "Adding a role",
      "prov:value": "> **This step said `summary` and `actorKind` until 2026-09-20, and both\n   > were wrong in the way that costs something.** `title`/`description` are\n   > the two labels EVERY knowledge-graph node carries — the reason is on\n   > `RoleDef.title` — and `actorKinds` is plural because the corpus settled\n   > it (see §\"An actor is one of three kinds\"). Neither is a spelling\n   > preference: **Zod strips an unknown key**, so a role written to this\n   > instruction type-checks, parses, and silently loses the field. That is\n   > bean `zdrf`'s failure class, and it happened — the three roles added in\n   > #453 each carry a `summary` that reaches no graph.\n2. Bind its lanes: each diagram declares `<bpmn:resource id=\"<role id>\"\n   name=\"<title>\"/>` once, and each lane that names this role carries\n   `partitionElementRef=\"<role id>\"`. The role lists no lanes, since a role is the\n   general node and a lane the dependent one (`data-modelling` step 8, #1168).\n   A lane with no ref is unbound, whatever its name.\n3. Give it the skills its lane's activities name. `role-carries-activity-skill`\n   fails if an activity demands something its performer was never given.\n4. `bun run cat kg:audit`. The kg-qa sidecars it writes are derived results whose\n   record the CI job `qa-publish` stores on the `qa-reports` branch; they are\n   committed only until bean `5hox` takes them off `main`.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "bean zdrf",
        "bean 5hox",
        "#453",
        "#1168",
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-33",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "Renaming a role, and a role's other names",
      "prov:value": "**Never let a name go silently.** A role carries every name it goes by\n(owner, 2026-09-30: *\"model both retired names and alternative names\"*):",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-30"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "skills/process/process-core/role-model.md#provenance-34",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "bean",
      "cat-harness:section": "A skill's `roles:` front matter is GONE — do not bring it back",
      "prov:value": "**What the removal did not decide.** Whether a skill *should* declare its\nperformer (bean `y1w9`), and what the tier words mean — if\n`reader`/`collaborator`/`owner` are forge permission tiers, that is a\ndeployment fact belonging to the topology axes rather than to a skill. Either\nway the field gets **declared before it is written**, which is the step the\noriginal skipped.\n`qif9` carries the four options; `folio-assistant-y1w9` (110 skills bound\nto no role or process) is blocked on the answer, because the obvious\nevidence for that triage is exactly this field.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "folio-assistant-y1w9"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
